Reading: with fast selection on capacity the joint dynamics reproduce the rate law: the certificate fails when the quasi-static
one does, and the seeded evaluation domain is captured shortly after; above the threshold nothing is captured. With slow
selection q lags q*, the failure is delayed and within the horizon may not occur, although the rate law says it does. The
threshold rate is not moved, but the failure TIME and so the finite-horizon necessity of R3a depend on how fast selection
tracks q*. Quasi-static capacity is the worst case for the certificate when q starts at or below q*.
